---
/**
 * EmployeeShell — THE ONE DOCUMENT EVERY EMPLOYEE-PORTAL PAGE RENDERS INTO.
 *
 * =================================================================================================
 * WHAT WAS WRONG, MEASURED
 * =================================================================================================
 *
 * Thirty-four pages under /portal/employee each opened with their own `<!doctype html>`, their own
 * `<head>`, their own header markup, and a `<style>` block of between 22 and 109 lines. They were
 * not one design re-typed thirty-four times. They were TWO designs, re-typed:
 *
 *   FAMILY A, 14 pages (attendance, timesheet, credits, schedule, evidence, calendar, internship):
 *     a light `<header class="topbar">` with an h1 and a subtitle, a horizontally scrolling tab
 *     strip, and the palette #F8F8F6 / #1B1813 / #FDFDFC / #DFDCD8. No back control at all — the
 *     only way out was a tab.
 *
 *   FAMILY B, 17 pages (payslips, wallet, documents, expenses, leave, support, projects, …):
 *     a dark sticky bar with a back link, and the palette #f6f4ef / #1a1510 / #fff / #ece4d6.
 *
 *   Plus three that were neither.
 *
 * So which product you appeared to be using depended on which page you had opened. On top of that,
 * inside each family the copies had drifted in every way a re-typed thing drifts:
 *
 *   - The back link said "Workspace" on some pages and "My HR" on others. One destination, two
 *     names, so the product appeared to have two places called home.
 *   - Half the titles ended in " - EduRankAI" and half did not — which is what a browser tab strip
 *     and a phone's task switcher show a person.
 *   - `.wrap` was 560 / 600 / 620 / 640 / 680 / 720 / 760 / 860px depending on which page was copied.
 *   - `padding-bottom` was 40, 48 or 96.
 *   - `.card h3` was #6b6255 on most pages and #9a8f7d on payslips. Nobody chose that.
 *   - Fourteen pages had NO <h1> anywhere in the document.
 *   - Not one page respected `env(safe-area-inset-top)`, so every sticky bar sat under the notch.
 *
 * None of those is a bug on its own. Together they are what makes a product feel unfinished, and
 * none of them is fixable in one place while the document lives in thirty-four files.
 *
 * =================================================================================================
 * ONE HEADER, NOT TWO. THE TAB STRIP IS A STRIP, NOT A SECOND SHELL.
 * =================================================================================================
 *
 * Family A's stacked header is gone. Every page in the portal now carries the same bar: back
 * control, page name as the h1, the person on the right. The tabbed sections keep their tabs — they
 * are genuinely useful, and a section of five related screens should look like one — but the tabs
 * are now a STRIP INSIDE THE COLUMN, under the shared bar, rather than the top of a different page
 * design. Those pages also gain a back control, which they never had: previously the only way out of
 * Timesheet was to pick another tab.
 *
 * The tab MARKUP stays in the page, deliberately. One page builds its tabs from a `.map()` over a
 * view list and another writes five literal anchors; turning both into a prop array would mean this
 * component owning a data shape it has no opinion about. It owns the STYLING (`.tabs`, `.tab`,
 * `.tab[aria-current]`), which is the part that was duplicated fourteen times.
 *
 * =================================================================================================
 * ONE PALETTE, AS TOKENS
 * =================================================================================================
 *
 * The `--emp-*` custom properties below are the whole palette, declared once. Family B's values won,
 * because paper/ink/rust is this product's design direction and Family A's #F8F8F6 was a
 * near-miss of the same idea. Every page's own CSS now refers to these tokens rather than to a hex,
 * so the next palette change is one edit here instead of thirty-four.
 *
 * =================================================================================================
 * WHAT IT OWNS, AND WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT
 * =================================================================================================
 *
 * IT OWNS the document — doctype, <head>, viewport with viewport-fit=cover, favicon, title,
 * noindex, the reset, the body base, the sticky bar, the tab strip, the column — and the palette.
 *
 * IT DOES NOT OWN a page's own components. Each page keeps its `<style>` block for the things only
 * it renders, marked `is:global` so those rules still reach <body> and the chrome rendered here. A
 * shell that tried to own every card in the portal would be a redesign, and a redesign is not what a
 * consolidation is for.
 *
 * MOBILE FIRST, because roughly nine in ten of the people using this are on a phone. 360px is the
 * design width, every interactive target clears 44px, the safe-area inset is respected top and
 * bottom, and the page never scrolls sideways.
 *
 * NO FLOATING ACTION BUTTON. These routes render their own document rather than going through
 * BaseLayout, so they never had one and none is added here.
 */
export interface Props {
  /** The page name. It is the <h1> in the bar, and the <title> unless documentTitle overrides it. */
  title: string;
  /**
   * Overrides the <title> alone, for a page whose tab name must differ from its heading — a project
   * detail page names the project while the bar says "Project".
   */
  documentTitle?: string;
  /** Where the back control goes. Defaults to the workspace home, which is where it always went. */
  back?: string;
  /**
   * The word on the back control. ONE default, and it is "Workspace" because that is the name of
   * /portal/employee everywhere else in the product — the nav, the composer, the widget registry.
   * "My HR" was a second name for the same place and is gone. A control pointing somewhere OTHER
   * than the workspace passes its own wording.
   */
  backLabel?: string;
  /**
   * The line on the right. Usually the person's own name; Family A used it for a short subtitle
   * ("Your week", "Your working day") and either reads correctly there.
   */
  person?: string | null;
  /** Column width in px. Sets --emp-width, which `.wrap` reads whether the shell or the page renders it. */
  width?: number;
  /** Space under the content. 96 leaves room for a sticky action bar; 48 is the plain default. */
  bottomPad?: number;
  /**
   * For a page that lays out its own containers. Every migrated page passes it, because each already
   * renders its own `<main class="wrap">` or `<div class="wrap">` — and a `<main>` landmark written
   * by the page is better than a `<div>` written here. The centred column is then not rendered by
   * this component and the slot receives the body directly; `.wrap` is styled either way.
   */
  bare?: boolean;
}

const {
  title,
  documentTitle,
  back = '/portal/employee',
  backLabel = 'Workspace',
  person = '',
  width = 680,
  bottomPad = 48,
  bare = false,
} = Astro.props;

// Declared before the markup that reads them — `const` is not hoisted, and a template reaching a
// later declaration has taken pages down on this project.
//
// ONE TITLE RULE. Every tab in this portal now reads "<page> · EduRankAI", so the tab strip and the
// phone task switcher stay legible when six of them are open. It was previously a coin toss.
const docTitle = (documentTitle || title) + ' · EduRankAI';

// The two per-page dimensions ride on <body> as an inline style and a custom property rather than in
// the stylesheet, because they are the only two values that legitimately differ per page and a
// stylesheet cannot be parameterised per render.
const bodyStyle =
  'padding-bottom:' + Math.max(0, Math.min(Number(bottomPad) || 48, 160)) + 'px;' +
  '--emp-width:' + Math.max(320, Math.min(Number(width) || 680, 1100)) + 'px;';
---
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en" data-surface="warm" data-theme="light" style="background:#f6f4ef;">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8" />
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1,viewport-fit=cover" />
  <title>{docTitle}</title>
  {/* Every one of these routes is somebody's own record. None belongs in a search index, and the
      pages that rendered their own document were each responsible for remembering that. Half did. */}
  <meta name="robots" content="noindex,nofollow" />
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="/era/era-tokens.css" />
  <link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="/favicon.svg" />
</head>
<body style={bodyStyle}>
  <nav class="top">
    <a class="top-back" href={back} aria-label={'Back to ' + backLabel}>
      <svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><polyline points="15 18 9 12 15 6"/></svg>
      <span>{backLabel}</span>
    </a>
    {/* AN <h1>, NOT A <span>, AND IT IS NOT COSMETIC. Fourteen of these pages had NO <h1> ANYWHERE
        in the document — so a screen-reader user landing on /portal/employee/payslips got a page
        with no heading to orient by, and the heading shortcut every assistive technology offers
        found nothing. The bar already displayed the page name; it was marked up as decoration. */}
    <h1 class="top-title">{title}</h1>
    {/* A trailing control, for the pages that put a sibling link here instead of a name. With no
        slot passed, the person's own name is shown. */}
    <span class="top-person"><slot name="trailing">{person}</slot></span>
  </nav>

  {bare ? <slot /> : <div class="wrap"><slot /></div>}
</body>
</html>

<style is:global>
  /* ===============================================================================================
     THE PALETTE, ONCE.

     Two were in use. These are Family B's values, because paper / ink / rust is this product's
     design language and Family A's #F8F8F6 / #1B1813 was a near-miss of the same idea rather than a
     different intention. Every page's stylesheet now names these tokens instead of a hex, so the
     next palette decision is one edit in this block.
     =============================================================================================== */
  :root{
    --emp-bg:#f6f4ef;          /* the page */
    --emp-surface:#ffffff;     /* a card */
    --emp-surface-2:#faf7f1;   /* an inset: a field, a quiet panel */
    --emp-ink:#1a1510;         /* body text */
    --emp-muted:#6b6255;       /* secondary text, labels */
    --emp-dim:#9a8f7d;         /* tertiary text, timestamps */
    --emp-line:#ece4d6;        /* a card border */
    --emp-line-2:#e6ddcd;      /* a field border, one step darker */
    --emp-accent:#c2410c;      /* the primary action. Rust, and it is the only accent. */
    --emp-accent-ink:#ffffff;  /* text on the accent */
    --emp-accent-deep:#7c2d12; /* the focus ring */
    --emp-bar:#12100c;         /* the top bar */

    /* Status. Family A and Family B each had their own near-identical set; these are one set. */
    --emp-ok-bg:#f1f8f3;   --emp-ok-line:#cbe3d2;   --emp-ok-ink:#1f5132;
    --emp-bad-bg:#fdf3f2;  --emp-bad-line:#efcfcb;  --emp-bad-ink:#8a2c21;
    --emp-warn-bg:#fdf6ec; --emp-warn-line:#f0e0c6; --emp-warn-ink:#7a5a2e;
  }

  /* ===============================================================================================
     THE BASE. Everything here was previously re-typed in each of thirty-four page files. A page that
     needs something different still says so in its own block, which loads after this one — this is a
     floor, not a ceiling.
     =============================================================================================== */
  *{box-sizing:border-box;margin:0;padding:0;}

  html,body{overflow-x:hidden;}

  body{
    background:var(--emp-bg);
    font-family:'Inter Tight',system-ui,-apple-system,'Segoe UI',Roboto,sans-serif;
    min-height:100vh;
    color:var(--emp-ink);
    -webkit-text-size-adjust:100%;
  }

  /* -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
     THE TOP BAR. Sticky, 54px, and it respects the notch — `env(safe-area-inset-top)` was missing
     from every one of the thirty-four hand-written copies, so on a modern phone the bar sat under
     the status bar.
     ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
  .top{
    background:var(--emp-bar);
    min-height:54px;
    display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:10px;
    padding:max(0px, env(safe-area-inset-top)) 14px 0;
    position:sticky;top:0;z-index:40;
  }
  .top-back{
    color:var(--emp-dim);text-decoration:none;
    display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:6px;
    font-size:14px;min-height:44px;
    /* The label can go if the page name is long; the arrow and the 44px target never do. */
    flex:0 1 auto;min-width:44px;
  }
  .top-back:hover,.top-back:focus-visible{color:var(--emp-bg);}
  .top-title{
    font-size:15px;font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-family:'Fraunces',serif;
    /* An h1 carries a browser default size and margin; the bar is 54px and neither is wanted. */
    margin:0;line-height:1.2;
    text-align:center;flex:1 1 auto;
    overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis;white-space:nowrap;
  }
  .top-person{
    font-size:11px;color:var(--emp-dim);
    overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis;white-space:nowrap;
    max-width:30vw;flex:0 1 auto;text-align:right;
  }
  /* Under 380px the bar has room for two of the three. The person's own name is the one they already
     know, so it is what goes. */
  @media (max-width:379px){ .top-person{display:none;} }
  /* A trailing LINK is a control, not a label, so it keeps its 44px target at every width. */
  .top-person a{color:var(--emp-dim);text-decoration:none;display:inline-flex;align-items:center;min-height:44px;}
  .top-person a:hover,.top-person a:focus-visible{color:var(--emp-bg);}

  /* -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
     THE TAB STRIP, for the sections that have one. Fourteen pages carried their own copy of this in
     three whitespace-variant forms.

     `overflow-x:auto` with `flex:none` children is what lets seven tabs live on a 360px screen
     without wrapping the strip onto two lines or shrinking the targets below 44px. The strip itself
     is full-bleed, which is why every tabbed page passes `bare` and renders its own column.
     ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
  .tabs{
    display:flex;gap:6px;
    /* INSIDE THE COLUMN, not full-bleed. Family A hung its strip off the viewport edge while its
       cards were inset by 12px, so the tabs and the content they switched between did not line up.
       Living in the column means ONE rule serves every tabbed page in the portal — there were five
       different implementations of this control (`.tab`, `.tabs a`, `.tabs a.on`, pill heights of
       36, 38 and 44, and three different selected colours). */
    margin:0 0 12px;padding:0 0 4px;
    overflow-x:auto;-webkit-overflow-scrolling:touch;scrollbar-width:thin;
  }
  .tab{
    flex:none;display:inline-flex;align-items:center;
    min-height:44px;padding:7px 14px;
    border:1px solid var(--emp-line);border-radius:999px;
    background:var(--emp-surface);color:var(--emp-muted);
    font-size:0.8125rem;font-weight:600;text-decoration:none;white-space:nowrap;
  }
  .tab:hover{color:var(--emp-ink);}
  /* The selected tab is the accent, not black — one accent in the product, and this is where a
     person looks to know where they are. */
  .tab[aria-current="page"]{
    background:var(--emp-accent);border-color:var(--emp-accent);color:var(--emp-accent-ink);
  }

  /* -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
     THE COLUMN. `--emp-width` is set per page on <body>, so one rule serves every width the portal
     uses instead of eight copies of `.wrap` disagreeing by twenty pixels.
     ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
  .wrap{
    max-width:var(--emp-width, 680px);
    margin:0 auto;
    padding:14px max(14px, env(safe-area-inset-left)) 24px max(14px, env(safe-area-inset-right));
  }

  /* A standfirst under the tab strip. Three pages used the old stacked header's subtitle slot for a
     whole SENTENCE rather than a name ("Ask for a day to be put right, and decide the ones sent to
     you"). A sentence does not belong in a 30vw bar with an ellipsis on it, so it moved into the
     column and this is what it is styled as. */
  .lede{margin:0 0 6px;font-size:0.8125rem;line-height:1.55;color:var(--emp-muted);}

  /* `outline:none` on a focused control with nothing put in its place is the single most common
     accessibility defect in this portal's hand-written CSS. Declared here so a page that sets it
     still leaves a visible focus ring behind. */
  a:focus-visible,button:focus-visible,input:focus-visible,select:focus-visible,
  textarea:focus-visible,summary:focus-visible,[tabindex]:focus-visible{
    outline:3px solid var(--emp-accent-deep);outline-offset:2px;
  }

  /* Somebody who has asked their device to stop animating things means it. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce){
    *,*::before,*::after{animation-duration:.01ms !important;animation-iteration-count:1 !important;transition-duration:.01ms !important;scroll-behavior:auto !important;}
  }
</style>
